# FPS meter

Open {{control:view.fps-meter.toggle}} to show a small meter at the bottom-right corner
of the application window. It stays anchored to the window as the viewport and
panels change.
Choose the same item again to turn it off. The meter starts off each time you
open N3 and does not change your document, selection, settings, or undo history.

{{image:fps-meter-menu}}

**App FPS** measures the rate at which N3 submits completed application frames
for presentation. The frame interval is the average elapsed time between those
submissions. It includes time spent waiting between frames, including idle time
and stalls. It does not measure GPU execution time, frames actually displayed
by your screen, or the delay between your input and its visible result.

The meter waits for enough frames to form its first sample:

{{image:fps-meter-waiting}}

While frames are arriving, the sample updates after at least half a second.
The display is labeled **last sample**: an idle editor keeps its last value
until another frame arrives. Enabling the meter does not keep an otherwise idle
editor drawing just to refresh the number. Orbit or animate the scene when you
want to observe its active frame rate; a low rate while doing nothing is normal.

The meter is a passive overlay. It does not take pointer or keyboard input.
{{shortcut:ui.toggle}} hides it with the rest of the UI; repeating the shortcut
restores it if it is still enabled. Hiding the UI does not disable measurement.

